CSS 2.1 17 tables as Chromium lays them out. 'border-collapse', 'border-spacing' (internal horizontal/vertical longhands) and
'table-layout' are properties: a separate table spaces its cells and its edges by the border spacing, a collapsed one by
nothing; the 'gap' properties no longer apply to tables. An automatic table layout sizes its columns from every cell's
min-content and max-content widths, column elements, percentages and spanning cells, and fits an automatic table width into
the available width (CSS Tables 3, as Chromium's table_layout_utils computes it); a fixed layout keeps the first-row
algorithm. A table has a shrink-to-fit width. Rows, row groups and columns take no padding, border or margin, and a row's
height is a minimum. Later rows may add columns, and a cell spanning past the last row ends there. What stays unrepresented
reaches SystemInterface::LayoutNotRepresented: collapsed borders beside a border, a header group after other rows or a
footer group before them, baseline-aligned cells, content outside the table structure, and columns after rows.
